1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 c5 { Rethink: what's the best response to c5? } 3. e3 Nc6 4. c3 Nf6 5. Nd2 e6 6. Ngf3 Bd6 7. Bg3 O-O 8. Bd3 { Bb5 is another feasible line here. See the Carlsen game from first video for reference } 8... Qe7 { Black is trying to set up for e5. How/ why? See the next line vs engine } (8... Qc7 { goal of Qc7 from Black's perspective is to still do e5, while making Ne5 unplayable. now he has added an additional defender for e5 - black's Q, B & N vs white's B and Pawn (if white plays Ne5) } 9. Ne5 (9. dxc5 { But, QC5 is a mistake from black because we can win a pawn now and open up the H file too } 9... Bxg3 10. hxg3) 9... Nxe5 10. dxe5 Bxe5) 9. Ne5 { Both white and black have 2 defenders each for e5, but white's one defender is a pawn - so the exchange at e5, if initiated by black, is going to win material for white } (9. O-O e5 { Stockfish's evaluation seems to like Bxg3 more than e5. But Eric suggested E5 as the classical move from black in this case and that equals black's position. Net net, for white, don't castle when e5 is playable. }) 9... Nd7 10. Nxd7 Bxd7 { Bxd7 is a mistake (see note on 15. g6). Qxd7 is better for black. } (10... Qxd7 11. dxc5 (11. Bxd6 { This line leads to a perpetual check - in the best case. So, dxc5 is probably a better line for white. stockfish still gives white +.6 in that position } 11... Qxd6 12. dxc5 Qxc5 13. Bxh7+ Kxh7 14. Qh5+ Kg8 15. Ne4 g6 16. Qg5 Qa5 17. Nf6+ Kg7 18. Nh5+ Kg8 19. Nf6+ { Perpetual check }) 11... Bxc5 12. Nf3 { If Qxd7 is played, one possible approach for white is to try to gain control of the e5 squar }) 11. Bxd6 Qxd6 12. dxc5 Qxc5 { It's apparently a key position, no mater how uneventful it looks! let's figure out how } (12... Qc7 { QC7 is the stockfish recommended move in this case. But note, even in that case there could be scenarios where it might make sense to sacrifice the Light-squared bishop. see this sub-line } 13. O-O Ne5 14. Bc2 Qxc5 15. Bxh7+ Kxh7 16. Qh5+ Kg8 17. Qxe5) 13. Bxh7+ Kxh7 14. Qh5+ Kg8 15. Ne4 { d5 is pinned! but now this knight is threatening Q capture or a potential checkmate with Ng5 } 15... Qb5 (15... g6 { G6 seems to be the best response for Black. But, white gains the d7 bishop after the exchange and is up a pawn + better defense of the king. BUT as the white, you must check position of the light-squared bishop before you go for the bishop sacrifice } 16. Nxc5 gxh5 17. Nxd7) 16. Ng5 Rfd8 17. Qxf7+ Kh8 18. Qh5+ Kg8 19. O-O-O { White's goal: king safety + put the rook on D file where the king can get pushed (see the other line) } (19. Qh7+ Kf8 20. Qh8+ Ke7 21. Qxg7+ Kd6 22. Nf7+ Kc7 23. Nxd8 Rxd8 { at this stage - white is up a Rook + 3 pawn (8 points), for 2 minor pieces (6 points). but stockfish is rating white only up by ~1 at this stage. So, this line is not ideal after 18. QG8 where white was up by 4.3 as per Stockfish }) 19... Qa4 20. Kb1 e5 21. e4 { White is trying to Open up the D file with e4. Recapture with d pawn will result in a forced mate } 21... d4 (21... dxe4 22. Qh7+ Kf8 23. Qh8+ Ke7 24. Qxg7+ Ke8 25. Qf7#) 22. Rd3 { Goal is to bring the white D rook to king side and join the attack } 22... Be8 23. Qh7+ Kf8 24. Qf5+ (24. Rf3+ { seems to be an inferior line as white is missing the forced mate }) 24... Kg8 25. Qe6+ Kf8 26. Nh7# *


